{
  "praise_line": "You began with: \"Two things really worked. First, when a student gave an answer, you asked why, and waited, and she explained her reasoning about the shaded parts. That pushed the whole class to think.\"",
  "wins": [
    {
      "behaviour": "Prompted student reasoning by asking 'why' and waiting",
      "evidence": "\"when a student gave an answer, you asked why, and waited, and she explained her reasoning about the shaded parts.\""
    },
    {
      "behaviour": "Explained the term 'numerator' clearly, using gestures and student repetition",
      "evidence": "\"your definition of the numerator was very clear. You pointed at the shaded parts on the board and said, \\\"The top number counts them,\\\" and the students repeated it correctly.\""
    }
  ],
  "concern": null,
  "try": {
    "move": "After the teacher agrees to try a change, ask them to name exactly where in the lesson they will use it and what success will look like, then hold silence and wait for their full response.",
    "evidence": "\"So your commitment is to use \\\"think, pair, share\\\" at least twice in your next lesson. I will come back in 2 weeks to see how it goes. Is that all right?\"",
    "instead": "Next debrief, after the teacher commits, follow up with: 'Where exactly in the lesson will you do this?' and 'How will you know it worked?' then wait without jumping in so the teacher states the if–then details in their own words."
  },
  "reflection_question": "How did your use of praise plus a single suggestion shape the teacher's willingness to make a concrete commitment?",
  "value": "heshima",
  "rubric": {
    "opened_with_specific_praise": true,
    "anchored_in_real_moment": true,
    "asked_and_waited": true,
    "one_improvement_only": true,
    "moves_not_teacher": true,
    "elicited_if_then": true,
    "righting_reflex_held": true,
    "disparaged_teacher": false
  }
}
